g/sys has the stat and terminal modules fully implemented, and pkg/testutils has the difftest and runner utilities—so all three packages for release 00.0 are actually present in the codebase despite what the status descriptions claim.\n\nNow I need to verify whether the existing implementations actually satisfy all the PRD requirements by cross-referencing what each package claims to implement against the formal specifications, though I'm constrained from reading the PRD files directly. Looking at the testutils package, I can see the test infrastructure covers diff testing, binary execution with timeouts, output comparison, normalizers, and expected file handling. For the magefiles requirement, the version command is implemented but the magefiles directory itself is excluded. The real gap appears to be in pkg/sys—the architecture describes signal handling, process group management, and process priority capabilities that I'm not finding in the current codebase, particularly around SIGPIPE, SIGHUP, and SIGWINCH handling.\n\nBut I need to be careful here: the architecture might be describing the full planned system, not just what's required for release 00.0. Looking back at the code status, I notice that rel00.0-uc002-format and rel00.0-uc003-sys both show \"not started\" even though source code exists for them, which suggests there's a mismatch between what's been implemented and what's been formally tracked as complete.\n\nThe same pattern appears with rel00.0-uc004-testutils and rel00.0-uc001-magefiles. And I just realized cmd/version already implements the magefiles requirement, so that's another task that appears done but isn't marked as such.\n\nThis makes me wonder if there's actually anything left to implement for release 00.0, or if the real issue is that the code_status field isn't reflecting what's already been built. I should look at what the PRDs actually require rather than relying on the status labels.\n\nLooking at the architecture, prd002-sys lists sev